<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#agora">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An agora was the central public space of an ancient Greek city, used for markets, assemblies and civic life. Greek-style cities with agoras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and gymnasia were founded across Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> after the conquests of Alexander. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, such cities spread Hellenistic urban culture across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agora.jpg" alt="Agora" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George E. Koronaios · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ancient_Agora_from_the_Areopagus_on_May_12,_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
